== A Use of Exhortation

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. A use #emph[of exhortation, to stir up everyone who has given his name to Christ to make conscience of every duty he commands, and of this one especially, which he has pressed on us so earnestly in his word, and encouraged us to by many sweet and precious promises.] This work of charity is #emph[wages,] and like #emph[Samson]’s #emph[lion] it carries honey in its belly. The man who, out of a principle of love and in obedience to God’s command, opens his heart and hand wide to the poor will find God blessing his stock and store and prospering all his undertakings. A #emph[secret curse] goes out from God on the uncharitable man’s estate, which blights all his plans and undertakings, so that they come to nothing. In the same way, a #emph[secret blessing] goes out from God on the charitable man’s estate, by which he thrives and prospers in the world, to the wonder of himself and his neighbors.]

It is observed that the richest merchants and citizens are those who trade boldly, while those who are afraid to venture their goods have only small returns. In the same way experience shows that the Christians who are most ready to supply the wants of the poor, boldly venturing their goods upon the waters, are the ones who thrive and prosper most in the world. Why then will any man be so unwise as to lose his riches for fear of losing them? Why will he not rather seem to lose them, so that he may truly find them? These earthly things are surely lost by keeping them, and kept by bestowing them well.

What remains now but that you#super[+] look about you for where you may lay out your money to your best advantage? Make diligent inquiry after the poor ministers and members of Christ; seek them more than they seek you#super[+]. For it is most certain that you#super[+] gain more by giving to them than they do by receiving from you. And you#super[+] are more indebted to them for receiving your charity than they are to you for giving it. While you#super[+] only relieve #emph[their bodies,] they feed #emph[your souls,] as in Prov 11:17: “A merciful man benefits his own soul,” which is refreshed by the mercy he shows to others. And while they share in a small quantity of your#super[+] outward good things, you will have the same in kind returned here sevenfold into your bosoms. And at last you#super[+] will be received into the kingdom of heaven, prepared for all those who have fed the hungry, clothed the naked, and done such works of charity to the poor members of Christ (Matt 25:35). So you#super[+] do not benefit the poor so much by #emph[giving] as you profit yourselves by #emph[receiving.]

Riches are a sheer uncertainty, like a flock of birds in a man’s field: he cannot say they are his because they sit there; for wealth “makes wings for itself and flies.” Now in dealing with uncertain things, it will be your wisdom,

- 1\. To make them as sure as you#super[+] can.
- 2\. To make the best use of them that you#super[+] can.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. What greater wisdom is there than to make sure? And what better way can be imagined to make your#super[+] estate sure than by putting it into good hands? And what hands are safer than the hands of the Lord? Put your#super[+] riches into the hands of God’s poor, and by doing so you put them into God’s hands.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[In the affairs of a state, the #emph[resident ambassadors] who are sent abroad to live in foreign kingdoms secure our peace at home. So what we give out and send out to the poor secures the rest at home. For as the poor beg of you, so they beg of God for you, that he would preserve and bless your store. Their prayers are sent up to heaven for your security.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. #emph[And what better use can you#super[+] make of your riches than to bestow a part of them on the poor and needy?] For it is the chief end of God’s giving more of this world’s goods to some than to others, that those who have the greater store should give out of it to those who are in want. This was #emph[typified] in the #emph[Israelites’] gathering of the #emph[manna.] Though it was rained down from heaven, yet the Lord would not allow those who had gathered more than was needed for themselves and their household to hoard up their surplus. He commanded them to share their abundance with those who did not have enough, so that the man who had the most should have nothing left over, and the man who had the least should have no lack. The apostle applies this to the giving of alms out of our abundance to those who are in want (2 Cor 8:14).]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[By sharing our riches with the poor, we will make them #emph[our friends,] both to give evidence for us of the truth of our #emph[faith] and #emph[charity,] and to beg a plentiful return on us. This is the advice of our Savior in Luke 16:9: “Make friends for yourselves by means of the mammon of unrighteousness, so that when you#super[+] fail, they will receive you into eternal dwellings.” Here by #emph[mammon] our Savior means #emph[the abundance of this world’s goods.] He called it the #emph[mammon] of #emph[unrighteousness] because worldly men ordinarily get it unrighteously, keep it unrighteously, and use it unrighteously. He hints at this so that his disciples might be all the more wary of it, and all the more careful about #emph[making themselves friends by it.] That is, they should so use their riches in relieving the poor that the good works done by them may give testimony of their #emph[faith] and #emph[charity,] which is the part of friends. Friends are ready to give good testimony of one another. The coats and garments that #emph[Dorcas] made for poor widows (Acts 9:39) were such #emph[friends.] But note what follows: Make friends for yourselves by means of the mammon of unrighteousness, #emph[so that when you#super[+] fail, they will receive you into eternal dwellings. When you#super[+] fail,] that is, when you depart out of this world, #emph[they will receive you into eternal dwellings.] Some refer this word #emph[they] to those who, being relieved, call on God to recompense their benefactors; but most refer it to the #emph[riches] which the givers bestow on the poor. Because of the evidence these riches give of the #emph[faith] and #emph[charity] of the giver, they are said to receive the givers into eternal dwellings.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Surely rich men have a price in their hands with which to purchase a good inheritance for themselves, if only they had hearts to make use of it. Though charity lays no claim to any merit #emph[ex congruo,] or #emph[condigno] \[of fitness, or of worthiness\], yet it will be plentifully rewarded by God both here and hereafter.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[I hope you#super[+] will pardon my zeal in pressing this duty of #emph[charity] on you so much. I assure you#super[+] it does not come from any design to take anything from yourselves and your children, but only to show you #emph[the surest and safest way of thriving in the world,] and how you may settle God’s blessing on your children, and so make sure to them the portion you leave them. I am willing to do you#super[+] a kindness, and I will do you a great kindness if I can only persuade you effectually to this duty. Whatever kindness I may do in this to those in need, I will do a greater kindness to you#super[+] who will be persuaded to help them in their need, inasmuch as it is more blessed to give than to receive. So bear with me if I not only offer you#super[+] a kindness, but press you so earnestly to accept it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The #emph[apostle,] knowing how reluctant rich men especially were in all works of charity, advises #emph[Timothy] (1 Tim 6:17) not only to #emph[commend] the duty to them, but to #emph[command] it. The apostle’s expression is well worth noting. He does not say ἀγγέλλε, #emph[declare] to them, but παράγγελλε, #emph[charge] those who are rich in this world, as they love their lives and would save their souls, to be rich in good works. As one wittily comments on those words of the apostle: If God should charge the #emph[rocks,] they would send out water; if the #emph[stones,] they would become bread; if the #emph[ravens,] they would feed #emph[Elijah]\; if the #emph[quails,] they would feed the camp; if the #emph[clouds,] they would rain down food from heaven on his poor people. Will you#super[+] then be more rocky than rocks? More stony than stones? More ravenous than ravens? More senseless than birds? More empty than clouds?]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[If you#super[+] are rich in this world’s goods and are not rich in good works, do not talk of your #emph[faith,] for there can be no true faith without good works (Jas 2:17). And do not tell me of your#super[+] religion, for there can be no true religion in you as long as you make no conscience of this duty. “Pure … religion,” says the apostle #emph[James] (Jas 1:27), “is this: to care for orphans and widows in their distress”; and you#super[+] never learned any other religion from us.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[The Lord does indeed give us leave to eat and drink, and to cheer our hearts by enjoying, in some measure, the portion he has given us. But he never allowed us to keep it all to ourselves, or to spend it in gratifying our sinful lusts. He commands us to set apart some portion of our estate for the relief of those for whom nothing is provided. Prov 3:9: “Honor the #smallcaps[Lord] with your wealth,” says the wise man. He does not say, #emph[Honor yourself with your riches, but honor the Lord with them.] As they come from his grace, so they should be used for his glory. Now we use our riches for God’s glory when, in obedience to his command, we supply with them the wants of his children, who will be stirred up by this to bless and praise the name of God for his fatherly care and providence over them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Do I need to tell you yet again that your liberality to the poor cannot bring any loss to your estate, since the more you give, the more you will receive? It is told in fable of #emph[Midas] that #emph[whatever he touched was turned into gold.] But it is no fable or fancy that the hand of charity can do it: it can draw grace out of your#super[+] goods, righteousness out of riches, and heaven out of earth. Giving away goods to such good uses, while it seems to #emph[impair] what you#super[+] have, mightily #emph[improves] it, to your most singular advantage. The more liberal any man is, the more likely he is to be a rich man. The mercy of God will crown his kindness with such a blessing of store that he will find God will never be behindhand with him. What the poor receive from him is nothing in comparison with the blessing he will receive from the Lord. It may be that you do not find your store increased at once by it. Yet if you diligently observe the workings of God’s providence toward you, you will sooner or later find yourself abundantly repaid for all that you have paid out.]]
